	('a531b1f6-40be-4fef-82bb-d2942eac6e9f', '08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3', '975ee44e-1aec-4f10-9d88-ba5f5a2f0801', 'user', 'Ford has been a bit sick. Should we adjust wake windows at all?', '2026-04-11 11:42:13.350868+00', '23c65d8a-df88-47fe-aa66-4f1c5090fe6a'),
	('6f1cdbb9-a523-4521-8b89-8ac9e3fde3e1', '08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3', '975ee44e-1aec-4f10-9d88-ba5f5a2f0801', 'assistant', 'Good morning! I''m sorry to hear Ford isn''t feeling well — that''s tough on everyone. 💛

When babies are sick, it''s totally normal for them to need **a little more sleep** and **shorter wake windows**. Their bodies are working hard to recover! Here''s what I''d suggest:

## Adjustments While Ford Is Sick

- **Shorten wake windows by ~15–20 minutes.** So instead of his usual ~2h 30m–2h 50m range, aim for closer to **2h 10m–2h 30m** and watch his cues closely. He may show tired signs earlier than usual.

- **Be flexible on nap length.** If he sleeps longer than usual, that''s okay — his body needs it right now. I''d still loosely keep an eye on the nap budget, but don''t stress about capping naps as strictly as you normally would.

- **Stick with 2 naps** if possible, but if he''s really struggling and naps are short, a brief 3rd catnap is forgivable during illness.

- **Bedtime can come earlier too.** If he''s had a rough day, don''t hesitate to push bedtime to **6:30–6:45 PM** instead of 7:00.

## For Today

Since he woke at **6:40 AM**, I''d watch for sleepy cues around **8:50–9:10 AM** for that first nap.

The biggest thing: **follow his lead right now.** Once he''s feeling better, you can tighten things back up. How''s he doing otherwise — any fever, congestion?', '2026-04-11 11:42:27.440062+00', '23c65d8a-df88-47fe-aa66-4f1c5090fe6a'),
