stretch of night sleep (7h 11m initial block). Today he''s had one solid 1h 3m nap, so we want one more nap capped to keep total daytime sleep around 1h 45m–2h 3m, which mirrors the pattern preceding his better nights.

I''m recommending a wake window of 2h 45m (165 min) rather than his observed average of 3h 30m for several reasons: (1) age-appropriate range for a 7-month-old is 2h 15m–3h, and his long windows appear to be contributing to overtiredness and fragmented nights; (2) the parent targets 2 naps and a 6:30 PM earliest bedtime, so we need enough runway after Nap 2 for a proper last wake window before bed; (3) a slightly shorter mid-day window helps prevent the accumulation of sleep pressure that leads to rough nights. The cap of 60 minutes gives Ford room to get a good restorative nap (his Nap 1 lengths are often ~1h) while keeping total day nap at ~2h 3m max, well within the 3h budget and close to the ~1.5–2h total that preceded his best nights.

Rest-of-day plan: Nap 2 starts at 3:05 PM, capped at 60 min (wake by 4:05 PM at the latest; expected ~45 min based on his patterns so wake ~3:50 PM). Then a last wake window of ~2h 40m–2h 45m lands bedtime around 6:35 PM, just past the parent''s 6:30 PM earliest bedtime. If he sleeps the full 60-min cap, bedtime shifts to ~6:50 PM, which is still excellent. This schedule mirrors his April 1 pattern (2 naps, moderate total day sleep, bedtime ~7 PM range) that produced his best consolidated night sleep.', '2026-04-02 18:17:45.582209+00', 'e5133c87-87b2-4691-9081-52cf0be6bc72'),
