ly documented with high scores, but the harder nights followed days with short total nap time (33 min) or inconsistent schedules. The nights with ~2h 24m–2h 37m of daytime sleep and 2 naps seem to be the norm. His recent first naps tend to land around 9:17–10:05 AM with wake times around 6–7 AM, suggesting a first wake window of roughly 2h 45m–3h 15m. Given he woke at 6:04 AM today and had a fragmented night (multiple wakings totaling ~9h 44m of night sleep across several blocks), he may be slightly overtired, so I''m targeting the shorter end of his observed range — about 3h 10m — landing at 9:15 AM.

For the nap cap, Ford''s first naps recently run 1h 7m to 1h 28m. The parent prefers longer naps, so I''m setting the cap at 100 minutes (1h 40m) — generous enough to allow a long restorative nap but leaving ~80 minutes of budget for Nap 2. This keeps total daytime sleep around 2h 40m–3h, which aligns with the 2-nap day patterns that preceded his nights. Setting the cap higher than his average but below the full budget ensures we don''t blow the schedule.

For the rest of the day: If Nap 1 runs the full 100 minutes, Ford wakes at 10:55 AM. A second wake window of ~2h 45m puts Nap 2 start at 1:40 PM. Capping Nap 2 at 80 minutes (wake by 3:00 PM) leaves a last wake window of ~4 hours to bedtime at 7:00 PM. Ford''s recent last wake windows have been around 3h 43m–4h 15m, so a ~4h final window fits his pattern well and aligns with the parent''s 7:00 PM bedtime target. If naps run shorter, bedtime can flex slightly earlier, and the parent is open to a slightly later bedtime if needed.', '2026-04-08 12:04:54.193198+00', 'e0793574-81aa-4e73-868c-6f2b1eb3f116'),
