ake windows (his average is ~3h 12m), but given that (a) he''s already exceeded his nap budget, (b) the parent''s target bedtime is 7:00 PM, and (c) his recent nights have been quite fragmented (multiple wake-ups, short consolidated stretches), pushing bedtime later risks overtiredness without any benefit. The nights that followed days with ~2h 24m–2h 37m of nap sleep were harder, so today''s 3h 5m of daytime sleep is actually reasonable and may support a better night.

The last wake window of ~2h 22m is slightly below his typical range (2h 15m–3h), but it''s within the age-appropriate range for a 7-month-old''s last wake window, especially after a short catnap (28 min). His third nap was essentially a bridge nap to get him to bedtime, and a 2h 22m wake window after a bridge nap is perfectly appropriate. Stretching to a longer wake window (e.g., 7:30 PM or later) would push past the parent''s preferred bedtime without clear benefit.

The rest-of-day plan is simple: bedtime at 7:00 PM. No more naps are needed — in fact, adding another nap would be counterproductive given the already-exceeded nap budget and the late hour. The parent is open to a slightly later bedtime, but 7:00 PM hits their target perfectly and gives Ford a solid opportunity for a long night stretch. The focus should be on consolidating night sleep given the recent fragmented nights.', '2026-04-09 23:43:41.406159+00', '671c0c72-2d10-45b2-96b6-5585c947907f'),
	('08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3', 'bedtime', '2026-04-10 00:00:00+00', '2026-04-10 00:15:00+00', 30, '2026-04-10 23:00:00+00', 141, '08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3:43acf056-2e84-4773-979d-0c9eeb8e3e11:2026-04-09T21:38:37.747+00:00|2314d335-3602-45f8-8cb2-0860427b4465:2026-04-09T19:10:00+00:00|e6db0da7-2634-4c0c-ac6a-51fb6d0ca11d:2026-04-09T15:52:12.511+00:00|9be2c935-bcd4-4505-9ea2-2b4b394ceee0:2026-04-09T11:11:27.888+00:00|a8b5eceb-1ab1-4ff9-b045-90a18cdb21e5:2026-04-09T09:35:00+00:00|f1a6bdc9-5551-4c0c-974c-b4311ecd865f:2026-04-09T03:54:00+00:00|ce17f07d-274a-4ed5-8d8d-c965441662a2:2026-04-08T19:38:38.15+00:00|ee9046c2-96ec-4df5-8d31-2bec6a4084b3:2026-04-08T15:30:15.242+00:00|c9bd4689-011c-4233-8686-80bb1205495d:2026-04-08T11:04:00+00:00|58c6b68e-07b4-425a-8ab6-31b6750e7347:2026-04-08T07:28:00+00:00:Ford should only do 2 naps per day.', '[{"time": "7:00 PM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 4 start"}, {"note": "Wake after 30m", "time": "7:30 PM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 4 end"}, {"time": "10:00 PM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 5 start"}, {"note": "Wake after 30m", "time": "10:30 PM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 5 end"}, {"time": "1:00 AM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 6 start"}, {"note": "Wake after 30m", "time": "1:30 AM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 6 end"}, {"time": "6:00 PM", "event": "bedtime", "label": "Bedtime"}]', 'Ford has already had 3 naps today totaling 3h 5m, which exceeds the 2-nap target and the ~3h nap budget. It''s time for bedtime at 7:00 PM, aligning with the parent''s target and giving a reasonable last wake window of about 2h 22m from his last nap ending at 4:38 PM.', 'Ford is 7 months old and the parent (and coach memory) clearly wants a 2-nap schedule. Today, however, Ford ended up with 3 naps totaling 3h 5m — slightly over the ~3h daily nap budget. Since all 3 naps are already completed and the nap budget is fully used (actually slightly exceeded), there is no room for another nap. The right move is to proceed directly to bedtime.

Looking at Ford''s historical data, his average wake window is about 3h 12m, but his recent last wake windows before bedtime have varied — on Apr 8 he had a last wake window of about 2h 22m (nap ended ~4:38 PM, bedtime ~7:00 PM), and on Apr 7 the last wake window was about 2h 44m. Today''s last nap ended at 4:38 PM, so a 7:00 PM bedtime gives a last wake window of 2h 22m, which is on the shorter side but reasonable given that (a) this is the third nap day and Ford may be slightly overtired from the fragmented schedule, (b) the parent''s target bedtime is 7:00 PM, and (c) the nights following 2-nap days with ~2h 24m–2h 37m total nap time scored lower — today''s 3h 5m is actually more generous, so we want to protect night sleep by not pushing bedtime too late.

The plan is straightforward: bedtime at 7:00 PM. This matches the parent''s preferred bedtime exactly. Going forward, I''d recommend working toward a consistent 2-nap schedule with wake windows closer to 2h 30m–3h to avoid the need for a third nap. Today''s first nap was nearly 2 hours, which is great for the parent''s preference for longer naps, but the short second nap (40 min) led to needing a third catnap. Capping nap 1 around 1h 30m and stretching wake windows slightly could help consolidate into two solid naps.', '2026-04-09 23:43:42.290118+00', 'c3ff9988-e6b5-40a5-924d-71ac8e6f29b9'),
	('08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3', 'next_nap', '2026-04-10 14:15:00+00', '2026-04-10 14:30:00+00', 105, '2026-04-11 00:00:00+00', 180, '08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3:8adad10e-21f5-433a-8603-acc92f8807c9:2026-04-10T11:15:00+00:00|bfd538d9-b39d-4ee6-9e22-bb9b9d2f2ec3:2026-04-10T08:51:43.721+00:00|43acf056-2e84-4773-979d-0c9eeb8e3e11:2026-04-09T21:38:37.747+00:00|2314d335-3602-45f8-8cb2-0860427b4465:2026-04-09T19:10:00+00:00|e6db0da7-2634-4c0c-ac6a-51fb6d0ca11d:2026-04-09T15:52:12.511+00:00|9be2c935-bcd4-4505-9ea2-2b4b394ceee0:2026-04-09T11:11:27.888+00:00|a8b5eceb-1ab1-4ff9-b045-90a18cdb21e5:2026-04-09T09:35:00+00:00|f1a6bdc9-5551-4c0c-974c-b4311ecd865f:2026-04-09T03:54:00+00:00|ce17f07d-274a-4ed5-8d8d-c965441662a2:2026-04-08T19:38:38.15+00:00|ee9046c2-96ec-4df5-8d31-2bec6a4084b3:2026-04-08T15:30:15.242+00:00:Ford should only do 2 naps per day.', '[{"note": "3h wake window after 6:15 AM wake. Wake him by 11:00 AM if still sleeping.", "time": "9:15 AM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 1 start", "cap_minutes": 105, "wake_window_minutes": 180}, {"note": "Cap wake — wake Ford by 11:00 AM to protect Nap 2 timing.", "time": "11:00 AM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 1 end (cap)"}, {"note": "3h 15m wake window. Cap at 60 min to protect 7 PM bedtime.", "time": "2:15 PM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 2 start", "cap_minutes": 60, "wake_window_minutes": 180}, {"note": "Wake Ford by 3:15 PM to allow adequate last wake window.", "time": "3:15 PM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 2 end (cap)"}, {"note": "3h 45m last wake window — longest window of the day, typical for 2-nap schedule at 7 months.", "time": "7:00 PM", "event": "bedtime", "label": "Bedtime", "wake_window_minutes": 180}]', 'Ford woke at 6:15 AM after a rough night (~10h 20m with a split). A 3-hour first wake window targets Nap 1 at 9:15 AM, capped at 1h 45m to leave room for a solid Nap 2 and a ~7:15 PM bedtime.', 'Looking at Ford''s data, his best nights (score ≥70) followed 3-nap days with ~3h 5m total nap and last nap ending around 4:38 PM. However, the parent and coach both want 2 naps per day, and Ford has been averaging 2 naps recently. His harder nights followed days with lower total nap (1h 30m–2h 24m), so we need to maximize daytime sleep toward the 3h budget on a 2-nap schedule. Last night was fragmented — a long stretch 7:25 PM to 3:51 AM, then a split with wake 3:51–4:20 AM, then only slept until 6:15 AM. That''s roughly 10h 20m of night sleep with disruption, so Ford may be slightly overtired but not drastically so.

For the first wake window, Ford''s recent windows range widely (2h–4h 21m), but his age-appropriate range is 2h 15m–3h. His first wake windows on recent 2-nap days have been around 2h 44m–3h 15m. Given the rough night, I don''t want to push too long, but I also don''t want to go too short and risk a short nap. A 3-hour first wake window (6:15 AM + 3h = 9:15 AM) is right in the sweet spot. I''m capping Nap 1 at 105 minutes (1h 45m) — this is generous to honor the parent''s preference for longer naps, and Ford has shown he can do long first naps (1h 57m on Apr 9, 1h 28m on Apr 7, 1h 11m on Apr 8). Capping at 105 min leaves ~75 min of budget for Nap 2, which keeps total day nap near 3h.

For the rest of the day: If Nap 1 runs the full 105 min, Ford wakes at 10:45 AM (cap wake). Then a ~3h wake window puts Nap 2 start at 1:45 PM. Nap 2 is capped at 75 min (ending by 3:00 PM), leaving enough budget and protecting bedtime. After Nap 2 ends at 3:00 PM, a final wake window of ~3h 15m lands bedtime at ~6:15 PM — that''s a bit early. To keep bedtime closer to the parent''s 7:00 PM target and the parent''s openness to slightly later bedtime, I''ll stretch the last wake window to about 3h 15m–3h 30m, targeting ~6:30 PM if nap 2 ends at 3:00 PM, or I can shift Nap 2 slightly later. Let me adjust: Nap 1 at 9:15 AM capped at 105 min → wake 11:00 AM (expected ~90 min, cap at 105). Nap 2 at 2:15 PM with a 3h 15m window, capped at 60 min → wake 3:15 PM. Then last wake window of 3h 45m → bedtime 7:00 PM. The slightly longer last wake window is justified for a 2-nap schedule at 7 months where last windows tend to be the longest.', '2026-04-10 12:01:40.718072+00', '2efba975-ffc7-412e-b5c0-fb2c8992a5f3'),
	('08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3', 'next_nap', '2026-04-10 14:15:00+00', '2026-04-10 14:30:00+00', 105, '2026-04-11 00:00:00+00', 180, '08a3dd1c-213c-4343-a6fd-d8a79275fcd3:8adad10e-21f5-433a-8603-acc92f8807c9:2026-04-10T11:15:00+00:00|bfd538d9-b39d-4ee6-9e22-bb9b9d2f2ec3:2026-04-10T08:51:43.721+00:00|43acf056-2e84-4773-979d-0c9eeb8e3e11:2026-04-09T21:38:37.747+00:00|2314d335-3602-45f8-8cb2-0860427b4465:2026-04-09T19:10:00+00:00|e6db0da7-2634-4c0c-ac6a-51fb6d0ca11d:2026-04-09T15:52:12.511+00:00|9be2c935-bcd4-4505-9ea2-2b4b394ceee0:2026-04-09T11:11:27.888+00:00|a8b5eceb-1ab1-4ff9-b045-90a18cdb21e5:2026-04-09T09:35:00+00:00|f1a6bdc9-5551-4c0c-974c-b4311ecd865f:2026-04-09T03:54:00+00:00|ce17f07d-274a-4ed5-8d8d-c965441662a2:2026-04-08T19:38:38.15+00:00|ee9046c2-96ec-4df5-8d31-2bec6a4084b3:2026-04-08T15:30:15.242+00:00:Ford should only do 2 naps per day.', '[{"note": "Wake by 11:00 AM if still sleeping", "time": "9:15 AM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 1 start", "cap_minutes": 105, "wake_window_minutes": 180}, {"time": "11:00 AM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 1 end (cap)"}, {"note": "Wake by 3:00 PM if still sleeping", "time": "1:45 PM", "event": "nap_start", "label": "Nap 2 start", "cap_minutes": 75, "wake_window_minutes": 165}, {"time": "3:00 PM", "event": "nap_end", "label": "Nap 2 end (cap)"}, {"note": "Flex to 7:15 PM if Nap 2 runs short", "time": "7:00 PM", "event": "bedtime", "label": "Bedtime", "wake_window_minutes": 180}]', 'Ford woke at 6:15 AM after a rough night (~10h 20m with a split). A 3-hour first wake window puts Nap 1 at 9:15 AM, capped at 1h 45m to leave room for a solid Nap 2 and a ~7:00 PM bedtime.', 'Looking at Ford''s data, his best nights (score ≥70) followed 3-nap days with ~3h 5m total nap and last nap ending around 4:38 PM. However, the parent and coach both want 2 naps per day, and Ford''s recent average is 2.0 naps/day. His harder nights followed days with lower total nap (1h 30m–2h 24m), so we need to maximize daytime sleep toward the ~3h budget on a 2-nap schedule. His recent wake windows vary widely (2h–4h 21m), but his age-appropriate range is 2h 15m–3h. His average of 3h 11m is slightly stretched for his age, and last night was fragmented (7:25 PM–3:51 AM, then 4:20 AM–6:15 AM = ~10h 20m total night with a 29-min wake), so he may carry some sleep debt. A 3-hour first wake window (on the longer end of age range but well within Ford''s observed patterns) balances not pushing him too far while allowing a 2-nap schedule to work.

For Nap 1, I''m capping at 1h 45m (105 min). This is generous — the parent prefers longer naps — and leaves ~75 min of budget for Nap 2. Ford''s first naps have recently ranged from 1h 11m to 1h 57m, so 105 min is a realistic upper bound that protects the schedule. If he sleeps the full 105 min, he''d wake at 11:00 AM. Then a ~2h 45m wake window (165 min) puts Nap 2 at 1:45 PM. Nap 2 gets capped at 75 min (bringing total day nap to 3h if both are maxed). Nap 2 ending at 3:00 PM leaves a final wake window of ~4 hours to a 7:00 PM bedtime — which is on the longer side but consistent with Ford''s observed last wake windows and the parent''s target bedtime. If Nap 2 is shorter (e.g., 45 min), that''s still fine: last nap ends ~2:30 PM, giving a ~4h 30m last window to 7:00 PM, or the parent could flex bedtime slightly earlier to 6:45 PM.

This 2-nap plan targets ~2h 45m–3h total day sleep, which is close to the best-night pattern of ~3h 5m. The last nap ending around 2:30–3:00 PM is earlier than the best-night pattern''s 4:38 PM, but that pattern was on a 3-nap day. On a 2-nap schedule, this is the best we can do while keeping bedtime near 7:00 PM. The parent is open to a slightly later bedtime, so if naps run short we can push to 7:15 PM.', '2026-04-10 12:02:46.1601+00', '5bdd22b5-73cd-4504-9eef-3c0dd15b9bfe'),
