h50m on others). His average observed wake window is ~3h3m, and age-appropriate range is 2h15m–3h. Today he woke at 6:15 AM, napped 9:05–10:07 AM (first wake window ~2h50m, which is typical for him), and is now awake as of 10:07 AM.

For the second wake window on a 2-nap day, I''m recommending ~2h33m (153 minutes), placing Nap 2 at 12:40 PM. This is within his typical range and slightly on the shorter side of his average, which is appropriate because on a 2-nap day the second wake window doesn''t need to be as stretched — we want him well-rested going into the afternoon. I''m capping this nap at 60 minutes to keep total daytime sleep around 2h2m. While this is below the ideal ~3h from his best nights, on a 2-nap schedule we can''t easily replicate the 3-nap pattern. Capping at 60 minutes leaves a reasonable last wake window and protects bedtime.

For the rest of the day: Nap 2 starts at 12:40 PM, capped at 60 minutes so it ends by 1:40 PM. Then the last wake window before bedtime would be about 3h35m (to bedtime at ~5:15 PM) — but that''s way too early. Instead, I''ll use a last wake window of approximately 3h20m–3h25m, which for a 2-nap schedule at this age is reasonable (last wake windows tend to be the longest). This puts bedtime around 7:15 PM, which aligns well with Ford''s recent bedtimes (7:00–7:25 PM range). Total daytime nap will be ~2h2m — not quite the 3h from his best nights, but the best we can do on a 2-nap schedule while respecting the parent''s preference. If nights suffer, revisiting the 3-nap option may be worth discussing.', '2026-04-10 15:07:48.981218+00', 'b5456bec-1464-4203-ab22-912f9edf70a5'),
