The key question is whether to squeeze in a 3rd nap or go straight to bedtime. The parent explicitly wants 2 naps only. Ford''s last nap ended at 1:15 PM. His recent wake windows range from 2h to 2h 50m, with an average around 2h 59m. If I were to add a 3rd nap, it would start around 3:45-4:00 PM, run ~30 min to 4:15-4:30 PM, then need another 2.5-3h wake window pushing bedtime to 7:00-7:30 PM. But the parent says 2 naps only. With 2 naps done, we go to bedtime. A last wake window of about 5h 45m (1:15 PM to 7:00 PM) is long, but for a 7-month-old transitioning to 2 naps, the last wake window is typically the longest and can stretch to 3-3.5h in a standard 2-nap schedule. However, Ford''s naps ended early today, so we''re in a tough spot. A 7:00 PM bedtime gives a last wake window of 5h 45m which is quite long. But looking at his recent bedtimes (7:25 PM on Apr 9, 7:00 PM on Apr 8, 7:13 PM on Apr 7), 7:00 PM is consistent. Given the early last nap end, Ford may show tired cues earlier — parents should watch for sleepy signs starting around 6:30 PM and could move bedtime up to 6:45 PM if needed. I''m recommending 7:00 PM to stay consistent with his established pattern and avoid an unrealistically early bedtime.

Note: The data shows Ford''s best nights followed 3-nap days with ~3h 5m total nap and last nap ending ~4:38 PM. Today''s 2-nap schedule with only 2h 2m total is a mismatch. If Ford struggles tonight, it may be worth reconsidering the 2-nap-only rule or ensuring naps are longer/later. For today, I''m honoring the parent preference and recommending a 7:00 PM bedtime.', '2026-04-10 19:37:23.729317+00', '1ecc1d5f-0f0f-4e0f-bf61-66732f68b3fb'),
